WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.184
<v Call-line supervisor>I want to talk about the morning callback window for

00:00:03.184 --> 00:00:06.367
<v Call-line supervisor>renewals. My staff keep hearing from people who work nights.

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:09.551
<v Call-line supervisor>All three calls come while they're asleep, and then their

00:00:09.551 --> 00:00:10.824
<v Call-line supervisor>case goes to review.

00:00:11.174 --> 00:00:14.563
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>I hear you. My worry is consistency. If one group

00:00:14.563 --> 00:00:17.952
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>gets special timing, others will ask, and pretty soon nobody

00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:19.646
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>knows what the rule is.

00:00:19.996 --> 00:00:23.584
<v Call-line supervisor>That's fair. Can I ask what the requirement actually is?

00:00:23.584 --> 00:00:27.172
<v Call-line supervisor>Is the morning window in statute, or is it practice?

00:00:27.522 --> 00:00:31.122
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>Let me be precise. The requirement is a documented good-faith

00:00:31.122 --> 00:00:34.722
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>attempt to reach the person, plus a written notice. The

00:00:34.722 --> 00:00:38.322
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>morning window started years ago because that's when the team

00:00:38.322 --> 00:00:39.042
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>was staffed.

00:00:39.392 --> 00:00:43.332
<v Call-line supervisor>So the standard is the attempt and the notice. The

00:00:43.332 --> 00:00:47.272
<v Call-line supervisor>timing is a habit. There's research on exactly this. In

00:00:47.272 --> 00:00:51.212
<v Call-line supervisor>a SNAP program, families given interview dates late in the

00:00:51.212 --> 00:00:55.152
<v Call-line supervisor>month were over twenty percent less likely to keep benefits.

00:00:55.152 --> 00:00:58.304
<v Call-line supervisor>The requirement didn't change. Only the timing did.

00:00:58.654 --> 00:01:01.746
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>That's striking. I'd have assumed people who really need coverage

00:01:01.746 --> 00:01:03.910
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>will find a way to call back.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.988
<v Call-line supervisor>Most people assume that. But when Social Security field offices

00:01:07.988 --> 00:01:11.717
<v Call-line supervisor>closed, the people who dropped out were mostly those with

00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:15.445
<v Call-line supervisor>less education and lower earnings. The hassle didn't screen out

00:01:15.445 --> 00:01:19.173
<v Call-line supervisor>weak claims. It screened out people with the least room

00:01:19.173 --> 00:01:20.292
<v Call-line supervisor>to absorb it.

00:01:20.642 --> 00:01:24.260
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>All right. Here's what I can support. Everyone gets the

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.878
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>same standard: a documented attempt and a written notice. But

00:01:27.878 --> 00:01:31.496
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>at renewal, anyone can pick a contact window. That isn't

00:01:31.496 --> 00:01:35.114
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>special treatment. It's one standard with more than one route.

00:01:35.464 --> 00:01:39.097
<v Call-line supervisor>That works. My team would also like to add a

00:01:39.097 --> 00:01:42.730
<v Call-line supervisor>text reminder. I'd want it reviewed first, though. Some people

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:46.000
<v Call-line supervisor>share phones, so the message can't mention health coverage.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:49.618
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>Good catch. Run it by the privacy contact, keep it

00:01:49.618 --> 00:01:52.885
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>generic and spread the replies across your team so it

00:01:52.885 --> 00:01:54.846
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>doesn't all land on one person.

00:01:55.196 --> 00:01:58.879
<v Call-line supervisor>And to judge it, I'll compare procedural closures and time

00:01:58.879 --> 00:02:02.562
<v Call-line supervisor>to first contact before and after. Those come straight from

00:02:02.562 --> 00:02:05.876
<v Call-line supervisor>the system. A count of calls made says little.

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:10.306
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>Agreed. If closures drop and the standard hasn't moved, then

00:02:10.306 --> 00:02:14.386
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>the old window was deciding outcomes that eligibility should have

00:02:14.386 --> 00:02:14.794
<v Policy specialist responsible for the renewal procedure>decided.
